<exercises xml:id="sol-1-1-Variables_and_functions" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
	<xi:include href="ex-intro.ptx" />
	
	<page>
		<exercise xml:id="sol-dog-food-variables-functions">
			<introduction>
				<p>
					A 32 pound bag of dog food costs $29.97, but an 8 pound bag costs $11.28.
				</p>
			</introduction>
			<task>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Identify and name the variables, including the units.
					</p>
				</statement>
				<solution>
					<aside>
						<p>
							Hint: Choose letters that help you remember what they stand for!
						</p>
					</aside>
					<tabular 
						xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude"
						left="minor" 
						bottom="minor" 
						top="minor" 
						halign="center" 
						width="100%">
						<row header="yes">
							<cell>letter</cell>
							<cell>=</cell>
							<cell>everyday words</cell>
							<cell right="minor">(units)</cell>
						</row>
						<row>
							<cell><m>W</m></cell>
							<cell>=</cell>
							<cell>weight of bag</cell>
							<cell right="minor">(pounds)</cell>
						</row>
						<row>
							<cell><m>C</m></cell>
							<cell>=</cell>
							<cell>cost of bag of dog food</cell>
							<cell right="minor">(dollars)</cell>
						</row>
					</tabular>
				</solution>
			</task>
			<task workspace="2in">
				<statement>
					<p>
						Which variable is dependent and which is independent? Explain how you know.
					</p>
				</statement>
				<solution>
					<p>
						I like to say the two possible sentences out loud and decide which one makes more sense to me:
						<ul>
							<li>
								<p>
									the cost of the bag of dog food <em>depends on</em> the weight of the bag
								</p>
							</li>
							<li>
								<p>
									the weight of the bag of dog food <em>depends on</em> how much it costs
								</p>
							</li>
						</ul>
						Since the first sentence makes more sense to me, I will say:
					</p>
					<tabular
						left="minor" 
						bottom="minor" 
						top="minor" 
						halign="center">
						<row>
							<cell><m>W</m></cell>
							<cell>~</cell>
							<cell right="minor">indep</cell>
						</row>
						<row>
							<cell><m>C</m></cell>
							<cell>~</cell>
							<cell right="minor">dep</cell>
						</row>
					</tabular>
				</solution>
			</task>
			<task>
				<statement>
					<p>
						What might a 16 pound bag of dog food cost? Explain the reasoning behind your guess.
					</p>
				</statement>
				<solution>
					<p>
						Definitely between $11.28 and $29.97. 
					</p>
					<p>
						Hmm. <m>16\text{ pounds} = \frac12 \cdot 32 \text{ pounds}</m>
						and <m>\frac12 \cdot \$ 29.97 = \$ 14.99</m>,
					</p>
					<p>
						and also, <m>16\text{ pounds} = 2 \cdot 8 \text{ pounds}</m>
						and <m>2 \cdot \$ 11.28 = \$ 22.56</m>,
					</p>
					<p>
						so probably between $14.99 and $22.56.
					</p>
					<p>
						My best guess is, about $20.
					</p>
					<aside>
						<p>Remember, it's better to be approximately right than exactly wrong!</p>
					</aside>
				</solution>
			</task>
		</exercise>
	</page>
		
	<page>
		<exercise xml:id="sol-riverside-rent-variables-functions">
			<introduction>
				<p>
					Rent in the Riverside Neighborhood is expected to <alert>increase</alert> 7.2% each <alert>year</alert>. 
					<aside>
						<p>
							These words tell me that rent changes, so that's one of the variables, and that time is the other less obvious variable.
						</p>
					</aside>
				</p>
				<p>
					Average rent for an apartment is currently $830 per month. 
				</p>
			</introduction>
			<task>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Identify and name the variables, including the units.
					</p>
				</statement>
				<solution>
					<aside>
						<p>
							Notice that the units on <m>R</m> are not just <q>$</q>, but <q>$ per month</q>!
						</p>
					</aside>
					<tabular 
						xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude"
						left="minor" 
						bottom="minor" 
						top="minor" 
						halign="center" 
						width="100%">
						<row header="yes">
							<cell>letter</cell>
							<cell>=</cell>
							<cell>everyday words</cell>
							<cell right="minor">(units)</cell>
						</row>
						<row>
							<cell><m>R</m></cell>
							<cell>=</cell>
							<cell>rent for apartment in Riverside</cell>
							<cell right="minor">($ <alert>per month</alert>)</cell>
						</row>
						<row>
							<cell><m>Y</m></cell>
							<cell>=</cell>
							<cell>time</cell>
							<cell right="minor">(years from now)</cell>
						</row>
					</tabular>
				</solution>
			</task>
			<task >
				<statement>
					<p>
						Explain the dependence using a sentence of the form <q><fillin characters="3" /> is a function of <fillin characters="3" /></q>.
					</p>
				</statement>
				<solution>
					<p>
						<m>R</m> is a function of <m>Y</m>, or in everyday words, rent is a function of time.
					</p>
				</solution>
			</task>
			<task>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Which number is a constant in this story:
						the percent increase (7.2) or the apartment rent (830)?
						Explain how you know.
					</p>
				</statement>
				<solution>
					<p>
						The actual monthly dollar amount of rent changes each month, so 830 is certainly not a constant.
					</p>
					<p>
						So, 7.2 is probably the constant, and that makes sense in the story <mdash /> I tihnk this is saying that every year the rent will be 7.2% more than it was the previous year.
					</p>
				</solution>
			</task>
			<task >
				<statement>
					<p>
						What is a realistic domain for this function? 
						That means, for how many years might this sort of increase 
						in rent continue? 
						Express your answer as an inequality.
					</p>
				</statement>
				<solution>
					<p>
						Certainly our domain should include <em>this</em> year, and probably the next <q><em>few</em></q> years. How many years is <q>a few</q>? Your answer might be different from mine, but maybe I'll say the next 10 years:
						<aside>
							<p>
								The sign <m>\leq</m> means <q>less than or equal to.</q>
							</p>
						</aside>
						<md>
							0 \leq Y \leq 10
						</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</task>
			<task>
				<statement>
					<p>
						What is the average rent expected to be in 1 year? In 2 years? In 3 years? 
						Note: <me>7.2\% = \frac{7.2}{100} = 7.2 \div 100 = 0.072</me>. 
						Try figuring it out.
					</p>
				</statement>
				<solution>
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\text{Start: } \amp \$830 \text{ per month}</mrow>
							<mrow>\text{1 year: } \amp 7.2\% \text{ of } \$830 = 0.072 \ast 830 = \$59.76 \text{ increase}</mrow>
							<mrow> \amp \Rightarrow \$830 + \$59.76 = \$889.76 \text{ per month after 1 year}</mrow>
						</md>
						<aside>
							<p>
								Be sure to use the new value <mdash /> that's how percent increase works.
							</p>
						</aside>
						<md>
							<mrow>\text{2 years: } \amp 7.2\% \text{ of } \fbox{\$889.76} = 0.072 \ast 889.76 \approx \$64.06 \text{ increase}</mrow>
							<mrow> \amp \Rightarrow \$889.76 + \$64.06 = \$953.82 \text{ per month in 2 years}</mrow>
							<mrow>\text{3 years: } \amp 7.2\% \text{ of } \$953.82 = 0.072 \ast 953.82 \approx \$68.67 \text{ increase}</mrow>
							<mrow> \amp \Rightarrow \$953.82 + \$68.67 = \$1{,}022.49 \text{ per month at 3 years}</mrow>
						</md>
						<aside>
							<p>
								Remember, <m>\approx</m> means <q>approximately</q>.
							</p>
						</aside>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</task>
		</exercise>
	</page>
		
	<page>
		<exercise xml:id="sol-rounding-variables-functions">
			<introduction>
				<p>
					Round each number up, down, or off to the precision indicated.
				</p>
				<p>
					<em>
						For a discussion of rounding, see 
						<xref ref="sec-0-1-Approximation_and_rounding" text="custom">Prelude: approximation and rounding</xref>.
					</em>
				</p>
			</introduction>
			<task>
				<statement>
					<p>
						My calculations show I need a cross brace around 9.388 feet long. 
						I want the board to be long enough, so <alert>round up to the nearest foot</alert>.
					</p>
				</statement>
				<solution>
					<p>
						<m>\underline{9}.388 \approx \fbox{ 10 \text{ feet} }</m>
					</p>
				</solution>
				<!-- prefigure number line here! -->
			</task>
			<task>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Gas mileage is usually <alert>rounded down to the nearest one decimal place</alert>. 
						What is the gas mileage for a car measured as getting 42.812 miles per gallon? 
						What about a car getting 23.09 miles per gallon?
					</p>
				</statement>
				<solution>
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\underline{42.8}12 \amp \approx \fbox{42.8 \text{ mpg} }</mrow>
							<mrow>\underline{23.0}9 \amp \approx \fbox{23.0 \text{ mpg} }</mrow>
						</md>
						(Yes, <m>23.09</m> is closer to <m>23.1</m> than it is to <m>23.0</m>, but notice that the instructions said to round <alert>down</alert>.)
					</p>
				</solution>
				<!-- prefigure number line here! -->
			</task>
			<task>
				<statement>
					<p>
						The original budget estimates for the new community center gym were 
						rounded to the nearest hundred (that means ending in 00), 
						so we want to round our bid of $148,214.79 <alert>to the nearest hundred</alert>.
					</p>
				</statement>
				<solution>
					<p>
						<md>
							\$\underline{148{,}2}14.79 \approx \fbox{\$148{,}200}
						</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</task>
			<task>
				<statement>
					<p>
						The population estimate was 4.2 million people, 
						but revised estimates suggest 4,908,229 people. 
						Report the revised estimate <alert>rounded appropriately</alert>.
					</p>
				</statement>
				<solution>
					<p>
						Our rounded value should agree in precision with 4.2, so we'll round to the nearest one decimal place.
						<md>
							<mrow>  4 \amp {,} 908 {,} 229 \text{ people} </mrow>
							<mrow>= 4 \amp {.} 908 \phantom{,} 229 \text{ million people} </mrow>
							<mrow> \approx 4 \amp .9 \text{ million people}</mrow>
						</md>
						
					</p>
				</solution>
			</task>
		</exercise>
	</page>
		
	<page>
		<exercise xml:id="sol-time-variables-functions">
			<introduction>
				<p>
					It's about time! 
					<aside>
						<p component="not-workbook">
							(Stories also appear in 
							<xref ref="time-2-variables-functions"/>)
						</p>
						<p component="workbook">
							(Stories also appear in 
							<xref ref="sec-1-1-Variables_and_functions" text="global"/> Exercises)
						</p>
					</aside>
					In each story, time is one (or both) of the variables.  
					Identify and name the variables, including units and dependence.
				</p>
			</introduction>
			<task>
				<statement>
					<p>
						The Nussbaums planted a walnut tree years ago when they first bought their house. 
						<aside>
							<p component="not-workbook">
								(Story also appears in
								<xref ref="walnut-tree-arithmetic-operations"/> and
								<xref ref="walnut-tree-algebraic-notation"/>)
							</p>
							<p component="workbook">
								(Story also appears in
								<xref ref="sec-0-2-Arithmetic_operations" text="global"/> Exercises and
								<xref ref="sec-0-7-Algebraic_notation" text="global"/> Exercises)
							</p>
						</aside>
						The tree was 5 feet tall then and has grown around 2 feet a year.
					</p>
				</statement>
				<solution>
					<tabular
						left="minor" bottom="minor" top="minor"
						halign="center" width="100%">
						<row header="yes">
							<cell>letter</cell>
							<cell>=</cell>
							<cell>everyday words</cell>
							<cell>(units)</cell>
							<cell>~</cell>
							<cell right="minor">dep or indep</cell>
						</row>
						<row>
							<cell><m>H</m></cell>
							<cell>=</cell>
							<cell>height of walnut tree</cell>
							<cell>(feet)</cell>
							<cell>~</cell>
							<cell right="minor">dep</cell>
						</row>
						<row>
							<cell><m>T</m></cell>
							<cell>=</cell>
							<cell>time since tree was planted</cell>
							<cell>(years)</cell>
							<cell>~</cell>
							<cell right="minor">indep</cell>
						</row>
					</tabular>
				</solution>
			</task>
			<task xml:id="bac-variables-functions">
				<statement>
					<p>
						After his first beer, Stephen's blood alcohol content (BAC) was already 0.04,
						<aside>
							<p component="not-workbook">
								(Story also appears in 
								<xref ref="bac-approx-solutions"/> and
								<xref ref="bac-solving-exponential"/>)
							</p>
							<p component="workbook">
								(Story also appears in 
								<xref ref="sec-2-4-Approx_solutions" text="global"/> Exercises and 
								<xref ref="bac-solving-exponential"/>)
							</p>
						</aside>
						and as he continued to drink, his BAC level rose 45% per hour.
					</p>
				</statement>
				<solution>
					<tabular
						left="minor" bottom="minor" top="minor"
						halign="center" width="100%">
						<row header="yes">
							<cell>letter</cell>
							<cell>=</cell>
							<cell>everyday words</cell>
							<cell>(units)</cell>
							<cell>~</cell>
							<cell right="minor">dep or indep</cell>
						</row>
						<row>
							<cell><m>S</m></cell>
							<cell>=</cell>
							<cell>Stephen's blood alcohol content</cell>
							<cell>()</cell>
							<cell>~</cell>
							<cell right="minor">dep</cell>
						</row>
						<row>
							<cell><m>H</m></cell>
							<cell>=</cell>
							<cell>time Stephen is drinking</cell>
							<cell>(hours)</cell>
							<cell>~</cell>
							<cell right="minor">indep</cell>
						</row>
					</tabular>
				</solution>
			</task>
			<task>
				<statement>
					<p>
						When McKenna drives 60 mph (miles per hour), 
						it takes her 20 minutes on the highway to get between exits, 
						but when traffic is bad, it can take her an hour.
					</p>
					<aside>
						<p>
							Because she has to drive <em>slower</em>, which means that her <em>speed</em> changes.
						</p>
					</aside>
				</statement>
				<solution>
					<tabular
						left="minor" bottom="minor" top="minor"
						halign="center" width="100%">
						<row header="yes">
							<cell>letter</cell>
							<cell>=</cell>
							<cell>everyday words</cell>
							<cell>(units)</cell>
							<cell>~</cell>
							<cell right="minor">dep or indep</cell>
						</row>
						<row>
							<cell><m>S</m></cell>
							<cell>=</cell>
							<cell>speed McKenna drives</cell>
							<cell>(mph)</cell>
							<cell>~</cell>
							<cell right="minor">indep</cell>
						</row>
						<row>
							<cell><m>T</m></cell>
							<cell>=</cell>
							<cell>time between exits</cell>
							<cell>(minutes)</cell>
							<cell>~</cell>
							<cell right="minor">dep</cell>
						</row>
					</tabular>
					<p>
						<aside>
							<p>
								Notice that her time <em>depends on</em> how fast she drives, so time <m>T</m> is the dependent variable here.
							</p>
						</aside>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</task>
			<task>
				<statement>
					<p>
						The sun set at 6:00 p.m. today, and I heard on the radio 
						that at this time of year, it sets about 2 minutes earlier each day.
						<em>(Hint: measure the sunset time in minutes after 6:00 p.m.)</em>
					</p>
				</statement>
				<solution>
					<tabular
						left="minor" bottom="minor" top="minor"
						halign="center" width="100%">
						<row header="yes">
							<cell>letter</cell>
							<cell>=</cell>
							<cell>everyday words</cell>
							<cell>(units)</cell>
							<cell>~</cell>
							<cell right="minor">dep or indep</cell>
						</row>
						<row>
							<cell><m>D</m></cell>
							<cell>=</cell>
							<cell>number of days from now</cell>
							<cell>(days)</cell>
							<cell>~</cell>
							<cell right="minor">indep</cell>
						</row>
						<row>
							<cell><m>S</m></cell>
							<cell>=</cell>
							<cell>time the sun sets</cell>
							<cell>(minutes after 6pm)</cell>
							<cell>~</cell>
							<cell right="minor">dep</cell>
						</row>
					</tabular>
				</solution>
			</task>
		</exercise>
	</page>

	

	<exercise xml:id="sol-time-2-variables-functions" component="not-workbook">
		<introduction>
			<p>
				It's about time! 
				<aside>
					<p>
						(Stories also appear in <xref ref="time-variables-functions"/>)
					</p>
				</aside>
				For each story, try to figure out the answer to the question(s).
			</p>
		</introduction>
					
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					The Nussbaums planted a walnut tree years ago when they first bought their house. 
					<aside>
						<p>
							(Story also appears in
							<xref ref="walnut-tree-arithmetic-operations"/> and
							<xref ref="walnut-tree-algebraic-notation"/>)
						</p>
					</aside>
					The tree was 5 feet tall then and has grown around 2 feet a year. The tree is now 40 feet tall. How long ago did the Nussbaums plant their walnut tree?
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
			
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					After his first beer, Stephen's blood alcohol content (BAC) was already 0.04 and as he continued to drink, his BAC level rose 45% per hour.
					<aside>
						<p>
							(Story also appears in 
							<xref ref="bac-approx-solutions"/> and
							<xref ref="bac-solving-exponential"/>)
						</p>
					</aside>
					Note that <me>45\% = \frac{45}{100} = 45 \div 100 = 0.45</me> What was Stephen's BAC after 1 hour? After 2 hours?
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
			
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					When McKenna drives 60 mph (miles per hour) it takes her 20 minutes on the highway to get between exits, but when traffic is bad it can take her an hour. How slow is McKenna driving when traffic is bad? <em>Hint: can you figure out the distance between exits?</em>
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
			
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					The sun set at 6:00 p.m. today and I heard on the radio that it sets about 2 minutes earlier each day this time of year. In how many days will the sun set at 4:30 p.m.? <em>Bonus question: in what month is the story set?</em>
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
	</exercise>

	<exercise xml:id="sol-temp-mpls-variables-functions" component="not-workbook">
		<introduction>
			<p>
				The temperature was 40<degree />F at noon yesterday downtown Minneapolis,
				<aside>
					<p>
						(Story also appears in <xref ref="temp-mpls-tables-graphs"/> and <xref ref="temp-mpls-modeling-linear"/>)
					</p>
				</aside>
				but it dropped 3<degree />F an hour in the afternoon. 
			</p>
		</introduction>
				
				
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Which number is a constant in this story: the temperature (40) or the rate at which the temperature dropped (3)?
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
				
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Name the variables, including units and dependence.
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
				
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					When did the temperature drop below freezing (32<degree />F)?
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
	</exercise>

	<exercise xml:id="sol-social-security-variables-functions" component="not-workbook">
		<introduction>
			<p>
				Mrs. Nystrom's Social Security benefit was $746.17/month when she retired from teaching in 2009. 
				<aside>
					<p>
						(Story also appears in 
						<xref ref="social-security-arithmetic-operations"/>, 
						<xref ref="social-security-tables-graphs"/>, and 
						<xref ref="social-security-modeling-exponential"/>)
					</p>
				</aside>
				She had taught in elementary school since I was a girl. Benefits have increased by 4% per year. 
			</p>
		</introduction>
				
				
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Name the variables, including units and dependence.
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
				
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					What was her benefit in 2012?
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
				
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					When will her benefit pass $900/month? A reasonable guess is fine.
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
	</exercise>

	<exercise xml:id="sol-stamps-variables-functions" component="not-workbook">
		<introduction>
			<p>
				Between e-mail, automatic bill pay, and online banking, it seems like I hardly ever actually mail something. 
				<aside>
					<p>
						(Story also appears in <xref ref="stamps-solving-linear"/>)
					</p>
				</aside>
				But for those times, I need postage stamps. The corner store sells as many (or few) stamps as I want for 44¢ each but they charge a 75¢ convenience fee for the whole purchase. 
			</p>
		</introduction>

		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Identify and name the variables, including the units.
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
				
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Which variable is dependent and which is independent?
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
				
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					How many stamps could I buy for $10? Try to figure it out from the story.
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
	</exercise>

	<exercise xml:id="sol-car-value-variables-functions" component="not-workbook">
		<introduction>
			<p>
				Sofía bought her car new for $22,500. 
				<aside>
					<p>
						(Story also appears in <xref ref="sec-5-4-Linear_vs_exponential" text="type-global-title"/>)
					</p>
				</aside>
				Now the car is fairly old and just passed 109,000 miles. Sofía looked online and estimates the car is still worth $5,700.
			</p>
		</introduction>
				
				
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Identify and name the variables, including the units.
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
				
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Explain the dependence using a sentence of the form <q><fillin characters="3"/> is a function of <fillin characters="3"/></q>.
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
				
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					What is a realistic number of miles for a car to drive? Express the domain as an inequality.
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
				
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Sofía wonders when the car would be practically worthless, meaning under $500. Make a reasonable guess.
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
	</exercise>

	<exercise xml:id="sol-name-vars-variables-functions" component="not-workbook">
			<introduction>
				<p>
					For each story, name the variables including units and dependence.
				</p>
			</introduction>
				
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					The closer you sit to a lamp, the brighter the light is.
					<aside>
						<p>
							(Story also appears in 
							<xref ref="lamp-intensity-using-equations"/> and
							<xref ref="lamp-intensity-solving-power"/>)
						</p>
					</aside>
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
				
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					The thicker the piece of fish, the longer it takes to grill it.
					<aside>
						<p>
							(Story also appears in 
							<xref ref="grilling-fish-using-equations"/> and
							<xref ref="grilling-fish-solving-quadratic"/>)
						</p>
					</aside>
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
				
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Wind turbines are used to generate electricity. The faster the wind blows, the more power they generate. 
					<aside>
						<p>
							(Story also appears in 
							<xref ref="wind-turbine-rate-of-change"/>,
							<xref ref="wind-turbine-approx-solutions"/>, and
							<xref ref="wind-turbine-solving-power"/>)
						</p>
					</aside>
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
	</exercise>
</exercises>
